+
+  <section id="background">
+    <p class="section-kicker">01 · Background</p>
+    <h2>The problem begins with who owns the work</h2>
+    <span class="skip-note">Know the mirror lifecycle already? Skip to “The narrow problem.”</span>
+
+    <h3>Deep background: a mirror is created in two phases</h3>
+    <p>Entire stores a repository in regional infrastructure. When the CLI asks for a mirror, the system first establishes a <strong>placement</strong>: a durable record saying which regional cluster owns the repository. The cluster then performs the slower work needed to make the Git repository usable. Those are different facts. A placement can exist while the clone is still being prepared.</p>
+    <p>The CLI therefore has to answer two questions in order: “Where will this mirror live?” and “Is that mirror ready yet?” The second question was already asynchronous before this pull request. After the server returned a placement, the CLI polled the mirror every two seconds until its state became ready or terminal.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">The lifecycle the user cares about</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">Example: place entireio/cli in the us-east-2 cluster, then wait until it is cloneable.</p>
+      <div class="flow">
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">CLI intent</span><code>entire repo mirror create<br>aws-us-east-2</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Placement</span><code>mirror_id = m_42<br>cluster = us-east-2</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Preparation</span><code>state = cloning</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Usable result</span><code>state = ready<br>ssh://…/cli.git</code></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <p>This distinction matters because the command exposes <code>--no-wait</code>. “Do not wait” does not mean “fire an unvalidated packet and exit.” It means “wait until placement is known, print its URL, and leave clone readiness to the caller.” Without a placement there is no useful result to return.</p>
+
+    <h3>The narrow problem: the first phase was still synchronous</h3>
+    <p>Before this change, the CLI called the legacy mirror-create endpoint and kept that HTTP request open while the control plane arranged placement. If the connection disappeared, cancellation could propagate into work that the user expected the server to own. The client also had no durable request identifier it could poll after losing the original response.</p>
+    <p>The server-side contract added by <a href="https://github.com/entirehq/entiredb/pull/3441">entiredb PR #3441 (coreapi: add asynchronous mirror request routes)</a> changes that boundary. Submission returns HTTP 202 and a <code>Location</code> header for a persistent request. The server can continue processing independently; the CLI follows the receipt until it contains a successful placement or a structured failure.</p>
+
+    <div class="split figure">
+      <div class="lane">
+        <span class="lane-label">Before: connection owns phase one</span>
+        <ol>
+          <li>CLI opens <code>POST /mirrors</code>.</li>
+          <li>Request stays open during placement.</li>
+          <li>Response finally returns a mirror.</li>
+          <li>CLI polls clone readiness.</li>
+        </ol>
+      </div>
+      <div class="lane">
+        <span class="lane-label">After: request record owns phase one</span>
+        <ol>
+          <li>CLI opens <code>POST /mirror-requests</code>.</li>
+          <li>Server returns <code>202 + Location</code>.</li>
+          <li>CLI polls the durable request.</li>
+          <li>CLI reuses the same clone-readiness poll.</li>
+        </ol>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <div class="callout good">
+      <span class="callout-label">The governing invariant</span>
+      <p>After a successful submission, placement work belongs to the server-side request—not to the lifetime of the submitting HTTP connection.</p>
+    </div>
+
+    <h3>Why this is a rollout bridge, not an endpoint replacement</h3>
+    <p><a href="https://linear.app/entirehq/issue/COR-1185">COR-1185 (CLI: submit mirror creates through the async mirror-request routes)</a> deliberately keeps both routes. A normal layered repository setting, <code>async_mirror_requests</code>, selects the new path. Its default is false, so existing repositories retain the synchronous behavior until a project or local override enables the new contract.</p>
+    <p>Once enabled, the choice is strict: the CLI does not probe for capability and does not fall back on a 404. A missing route is reported as a failure. In effect, that makes rollout errors visible instead of quietly converting an intended durability guarantee back into the legacy behavior.</p>
+
+    <pre class="code"><span class="com">// .entire/settings.json (shared project setting)</span>
+{
+  <span class="key">"async_mirror_requests"</span>: <span class="str">true</span>
+}
+
+<span class="com">// .entire/settings.local.json may override it for one clone.</span></pre>
+
+    <p>This setting uses the repository’s ordinary project/local layering rather than a special local-only loader. There is one careful exception: <code>entire repo mirror create</code> is a control-plane command allowed to run even when the local <code>.entire</code> directory is broken. If settings cannot be loaded there, the command preserves that exemption and uses the default synchronous path rather than failing on local state.</p>
+  </section>
+
+  <section id="intuition">
+    <p class="section-kicker">02 · Intuition</p>
+    <h2>Think of submission as getting a claim ticket</h2>
+
+    <p>The essence is familiar from coat checks and job queues. Handing over the item is short. The receipt is durable. Collection can be retried. The new endpoint does not make placement itself instantaneous; it gives the client a stable name for work that continues after the first connection ends.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">A concrete async request</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">The values are illustrative, but the state transitions match the generated API contract.</p>
+      <div class="timeline">
+        <div class="state"><b>1. queued</b><small><code>POST</code> returns 202 and <code>Location: /api/v1/mirror-requests/7b…</code></small></div>
+        <div class="state"><b>2. placing</b><small><code>GET Location</code> returns <code>processing</code>; the control plane still owns the job.</small></div>
+        <div class="state"><b>3. cloning</b><small>The request succeeds with mirror ID and URLs; the CLI enters its existing mirror poll.</small></div>
+        <div class="state terminal"><b>4. ready</b><small>The mirror reports ready; the command prints the contract-backed URL.</small></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <p>The API request has four states—pending, processing, succeeded, and failed—but the CLI translates those into user-facing phases: queued, placing, and cloning. That translation keeps server vocabulary from leaking into every caller while still making progress intelligible.</p>
+
+    <h3>One timeout budget crosses both polls</h3>
+    <p>A subtle design choice follows from the claim-ticket model: <code>--timeout</code> is not reset after each phase. On the async path, a single context begins before submission and covers submission, placement polling, and clone polling. If the user asks for a 60-second wait, the entire operation gets 60 seconds—not 60 seconds for each step.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">One wall-clock budget</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">Example only: the actual phase durations depend on the service.</p>
+      <div class="flow">
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">0–1 s</span><code>submit</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">1–12 s</span><code>poll placement</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">12–47 s</span><code>poll clone</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">47 s</span><code>ready within<br>60 s total</code></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <p>The command preserves the existing two-second clone poll cadence and its tolerance for fifteen consecutive poll errors—about thirty seconds. The placement poll uses the same bounded error pattern. This is especially important immediately after placement: authorization data can lag briefly, so an early 404 need not turn a successful placement into a failed command.</p>
+
+    <h3>The Location header is both a receipt and a trust boundary</h3>
+    <p>The CLI does not blindly fetch whatever URL appears in <code>Location</code>. It accepts only HTTP(S) URLs whose path is exactly a mirror-request resource with a UUID, and rejects user information, query strings, fragments, malformed paths, and scheme-relative hosts. That prevents a server response from turning the authenticated client into a general-purpose URL fetcher.</p>
+    <p>Cross-jurisdiction routing adds another wrinkle. A request may first contact one core, receive a 421 redirect signal, exchange credentials, and retry against the repository’s home core. The server can return a relative <code>Location</code>. The transport therefore anchors that location to the <em>effective</em> request URL after routing, so the poll goes to the same home core with the right token.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">Why Location canonicalization belongs in the transport</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">The caller sees one trustworthy absolute poll target after cross-jurisdiction retry.</p>
+      <div class="flow">
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Initial target</span><code>core A<br>control token</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">421 routing</span><code>home = core B<br>exchange token</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">202 response</span><code>Location:<br>/api/v1/mirror-requests/7b…</code></div>
+        <div class="arrow">→</div>
+        <div class="node"><span class="n">Canonical target</span><code>https://core-b/…/7b…</code></div>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <h3>Truthful output beats a plausible guess</h3>
+    <p>The legacy response tells the CLI whether the mirror was newly registered or already existed. The async success contract returns the mirror’s identity and URLs, but no equivalent “created” bit. The CLI therefore prints neutral language—<code>Mirror placed at …</code>—rather than guessing from circumstances. The URL is contract-backed; the creation history is not.</p>
+
+    <div class="callout warn">
+      <span class="callout-label">Important edge case</span>
+      <p><code>--no-wait</code> on the async path still waits through placement. It skips only clone readiness, because the placement result is what provides the mirror URL.</p>
+    </div>
+  </section>
+
+  <section id="code">
+    <p class="section-kicker">03 · Code</p>
+    <h2>The implementation keeps one orchestration path</h2>
+
+    <p>The code is easiest to read as five cooperating layers. The generated API client describes the wire contract. Settings choose the rollout route. One orchestration function owns the lifecycle. Poll helpers interpret remote state. Finally, both the one-shot command and wizard call that same orchestration function.</p>
+
+    <h3>1. Generate the contract instead of hand-writing HTTP</h3>
+    <p>The OpenAPI specification now includes submission and lookup routes for mirror requests. Ogen generates request types, response variants, state enums, client methods, and JSON codecs. Most of the PR’s raw line count is this mechanical boundary; the handwritten code consumes typed outcomes rather than parsing unstructured responses.</p>
+    <p>The consequential contract is small: submission can yield 202 with a request plus <code>Location</code>; polling yields pending, processing, succeeded, or failed; success contains a mirror result; failure contains a stable code, message, and retryability flag. See the <a href="https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/1d65df23a2d6e584c3b7e536b13bda3bad5b3d66/internal/coreapi/spec/core.openapi.json#L10234-L10440">mirror-request routes in the pinned OpenAPI source</a>.</p>
+
+    <pre class="code"><span class="com">// Conceptual wire exchange</span>
+POST /api/v1/mirror-requests
+→ 202 Accepted
+→ Location: /api/v1/mirror-requests/7b88…
+
+GET /api/v1/mirror-requests/7b88…
+→ { <span class="key">"status"</span>: <span class="str">"succeeded"</span>, <span class="key">"result"</span>: { <span class="key">"mirror_id"</span>: <span class="str">"m_42"</span>, … } }</pre>
+
+    <h3>2. Route at the command boundary, then forget the flag</h3>
+    <p>The command loads normal layered settings and copies the boolean into <code>mirrorCreateOptions</code>. A settings-load error is deliberately non-fatal here because this control-plane command is exempt from the local <code>.entire</code> guard. After that boundary, callers pass one options value; they do not each reimplement rollout logic.</p>
+
+    <pre class="code">opts := mirrorCreateOptions{noWait: noWait, timeout: timeout}
+if settings, err := LoadEntireSettings(cmd.Context()); err == nil {
+    opts.async = settings.AsyncMirrorRequests
+}
+
+outcome, err := createAndAwaitMirror(ctx, client, owner, repo, cluster, opts)</pre>
+
+    <p>The setting itself is merged by the same scalar-setting machinery as other booleans. A local false can override a project true, which matters when one developer or test environment must remain on the legacy route. The behavior is pinned by the <a href="https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/1d65df23a2d6e584c3b7e536b13bda3bad5b3d66/cmd/entire/cli/settings/settings_test.go#L667-L693">layered-settings test</a>.</p>
+
+    <h3>3. Extend the lifecycle without duplicating its second half</h3>
+    <p><a href="https://github.com/entireio/cli/blob/1d65df23a2d6e584c3b7e536b13bda3bad5b3d66/cmd/entire/cli/repo_mirror.go#L573-L660"><code>createAndAwaitMirror</code></a> is the center of the change. It selects only phase one: async submits and awaits a placement; sync calls the existing create method. Both branches then converge on the old clone-readiness poll and the same <code>--no-wait</code> behavior.</p>
+
+    <div class="figure">
+      <p class="figure-title">Control flow inside createAndAwaitMirror</p>
+      <p class="figure-note">The branch is narrow; placement results are normalized before common behavior resumes.</p>
+      <div class="split">
+        <div class="lane">
+          <span class="lane-label">Setting false · legacy</span>
+          <ol>
